      "url": "https://www.last.fm/music/Kate+Bush/Aerial",
      "wiki_content": "Aerial is the eighth album by the English singer-songwriter and musician Kate Bush, released as double album in 2005, twelve years after her 1993 album The Red Shoes.\n\nThe second disc, subtitled A Sky of Honey, consists of a single piece of music revelling in the experience of outdoor adventures on a single summer day, beginning in the morning and ending twenty-four hours later with the next sunrise.\n\nAerial is Bush's first double album, and was released after a twelve-year absence from the music industry during which Bush devoted her time to family and the raising of her son, Bertie. The anticipation leading up to the album's release was immense, with press articles devoted to Bush being printed months, even years before. Like Bush's previous album, The Red Shoes, Aerial does not feature a cover photograph of Bush, but rather one that is emblematic of the album's celebration of sky, sea, and birdsong. The cover image, which seems to show a mountain range at sunset reflected on the sea is in fact a waveform of a blackbird song superimposed over a glowing photograph. <a href=\"https://www.last.fm/music/Kate+Bush/Aerial\">Read more on Last.fm</a>. User-contributed text is available under the Creative Commons By-SA License; additional terms may apply.",
      "wiki_summary": "Aerial is the eighth album by the English singer-songwriter and musician Kate Bush, released as double album in 2005, twelve years after her 1993 album The Red Shoes. The second disc, subtitled A Sky of Honey, consists of a single piece of music revelling in the experience of outdoor adventures on a single summer day, beginning in the morning and ending twenty-four hours later with the next sunrise. Aerial is Bush's first double album, and was released after a twelve-year absence from the music industry during which Bush devoted her time to family and the raising of her son, Bertie. The anticipation leading up to the album's <a href=\"https://www.last.fm/music/Kate+Bush/Aerial\">Read more on Last.fm</a>."
    },
    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Aerial is the eighth studio album by Kate Bush, released by EMI in 2005 as her first double album after a 12-year gap following The Red Shoes. It reached No. 3 in the UK, and its long gestation helped make it one of the most anticipated and widely discussed releases of Bush’s career.\n\nMusically, the album blends rock and pop with a wide range of textures, from folk and classical influences to more experimental and atmospheric writing. It is divided into two contrasting parts, A Sea of Honey and A Sky of Honey, the latter unfolding as a conceptual suite shaped by recurring images of birdsong, light, and the passing of a day.\n\nCritics often praised Aerial for its warmth, detail, and domestic themes, with many regarding it as a major late-career statement. Its mix of accessible songwriting and ambitious structure shows Bush balancing intimate, everyday imagery with a spacious, cinematic sense of scale.",
